																		Out-of-Canon hub where fictional characters are SCP-fied?
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/agentax" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5605511); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5605511&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608108821" alt="AgentAX" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5605511)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/agentax" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5605511); return false;" >AgentAX</a></span> <span class="odate time_1602331120 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Oct 2020 11:58</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4792742">
									

<p>What I'm proposing is essentially a separate section of the site, like the -J and -EX skips, where users will be able to submit SCP-fied fictional characters. Say, someone may want to make a file on Pennywise. As per site polilicy, a straight-up inclusion of his character cannot be made a mainlist SCP. They also can't (probably) rewrite him into something different, as was done with Chtulu. With this additional hub though, they can just turn him into SCP form.</p>
<p>Additions to this hub will have to be submitted into the Ideas Forum, be greenlighted and then go through the Draft Forum as well, to ensure their quality. I assume that an additional critics team may also be needed, to ensure that no low-effort posts are made (it's easy to write slopy conprocs and an uninteresting file and assume that just &quot;cause it's Godzilla people will like it&quot;).</p>
<p><strong>Edit:</strong> Because the main issue here seems to (understandably) be the legal complications, I thought I'd leave these links here. I'm absolutely not an expert and if people with legal experience say that it can't be done, it can't. But :</p>
<ul>
<li>I think that a &quot;parody&quot; of a copyrighted character is considered <a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fair_use">Fair Use</a>.</li>
<li>An SCP article presenting a known, existing character as a prisoner in a Site would most likely be considered a <a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legal_issues_with_fan_fiction">Fan Fiction</a> or <a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transformation_(law)">transformative</a> work. Both cases are considered to be fair use.</li>
<li>There is legal precedent of fanfictions being recognized (transformative works)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Edit 2:</strong> Argument debunked, too many legal issues.</p>

