								<div class="title" id="post-title-4793411">
																		Re: Out-of-mainlist hub where fictional characters are SCP-fied?
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/n-aepic-fael" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4463776); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4463776&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608108821" alt="N_Aepic_Fael" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4463776)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/n-aepic-fael" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4463776); return false;" >N_Aepic_Fael</a></span> <span class="odate time_1602421635 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Oct 2020 13:07</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4793411">
									

<p>Part of the reason why fanfiction tends to be ignored is that they're rarely, if ever, meant to be produced in a commercial setting. You're just begging for a legal liability here.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The only parallel I can think about where this works is those wikia fan pages, but again: those are licensed, for the most part under a non-commercial license. Corporations and studios don't need to fear someone is making money off of their work in that regard. The Wiki's license is inherently a commercial one, and making a hub for all this crap is inviting someone to turn it into merch.</p>
<p>Parody implies providing commentary into the character in question, but all you're doing here is just reframing the characters in a scipified manner There isn't much of a fair use argument here - you're not offering commentary on the copyrighted work in a meaningful way, but rather to satiate a desire to see things &quot;scipified.&quot;</p>
<p>Finally, you yourself posted a link to the legal issues to fanfiction:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Works of fanfiction are more likely to constitute fair use if they are &quot;transformative&quot; with respect to the original work, if they are non-commercial, if they appropriate relatively little of the original work, and/or if they do not tend to detract from the potential market for or value of the original work.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This isn't transformative in the slightest: you're effectively translating a character and making it fit the confines of the SCP universe. We fail the second point by default. Third claim goes out the window when you're taking a character wholesale and keeping their powers and personalities for the sake of scipifying them. The strongest claim that you have is the last point and even so, that's not appropriate: we've seen SCP entering the mainstream, with MrKlay's 096 film, and the popularity of Control giving us attention. Companies will eventually notice if someone were to make SCPennywise shit. Not only that, that very page you linked also says:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A derivative work is any work, including fanfiction, based upon one or more preexisting works. In the case where a copyright owner chooses to exercise their exclusive right to prepare derivative works against a work of fanfiction, they can sue the fanfiction writer for copyright infringement.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This puts us at the discretion of a copyright holder. And this is just under the US. We're an international group, and not everyone's laws are the same. Hell, the international part of the link says that one author is fine with fanfiction <em>so long as it is expressly non-commercial</em> which, again, the site cannot be due to CC BY-SA 3.0 being an inherent commercial license.</p>
<p>You can't point to Japan, Russia, China, or even Britain's leniency with fanfiction as an argument either because, again, this is an international community. Eventually, people would start putting in characters that would be under a more stringent nation's copyright laws, and it'd be stupid as hell as to limit what characters you're allowed to use in your proposed hub, as it defeats the whole purpose of it in the first place.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

